# Optimized Pong kernel — defconfig fragment
#
#   scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh -m defconfig \
#       patches/kernel-optimized/pong_optimized.fragment
#
# NOTE: the base is the *root-level* `defconfig` (7537 lines), which is what
# build_kernel.sh copies to .config. It is NOT arch/arm64/configs/defconfig —
# that one expands to a generic config with no MGLRU, WALT or zram, and is
# not what built r45b2.
#
# Verified against arter97/android_kernel_nothing_sm8475 @ master
# (906ca150c6ae = r45b2, matches the shipped boot.img banner). Every symbol
# below was checked for existence in the tree's Kconfig, and the whole
# fragment was test-merged + expanded with `olddefconfig`.
#
# [have] already set in the base, asserted so a rebase can't silently drop it
# [add]  genuine change vs the base
# [opt]  deliberate divergence from arter97 — read the note before enabling

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Memory reclaim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [have] MGLRU. arter97 backported it himself (182 lru_gen hits in mm/vmscan.c);
# it is NOT in ACK android12-5.10. LRU_GEN_WALKS_MMU is def_bool and comes for
# free. There is NO CONFIG_LRU_GEN_ENABLED in this tree, so MGLRU is compiled
# in but OFF at boot — enable it at runtime:
#     echo y > /sys/kernel/mm/lru_gen/enabled
CONFIG_LRU_GEN=y

# [add] DAMON. arter97 explicitly disables it (`# CONFIG_DAMON is not set`)
# and the binary has zero DAMON symbols — the widely-repeated claim that his
# kernel enables DAMON comes from a stale embedded config. The code is in the
# tree (mm/damon/), so this is a config flip. Verified to survive the merge.
CONFIG_DAMON=y
CONFIG_DAMON_PADDR=y
# CONFIG_DAMON_VADDR is not set
CONFIG_DAMON_RECLAIM=y

# [have] zram, writeback already off — matches arter97's reasoning that a
# 12 GB device gains nothing and pays storage-backed stalls for it.
CONFIG_ZRAM=y
CONFIG_ZSMALLOC=y
# CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qualcomm scheduler / power / thermal stack  — LEFT AS MODULES ON PURPOSE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All of these exist in the tree and are `=m` in arter97's config. An earlier
# revision of this fragment forced them to =y; the test merge showed that
# overrides ten vendor symbols at once. Building vendor drivers into the Image
# while vendor_dlkm still ships the matching .ko is how you get duplicate
# driver init and a boot loop, so the =m layout is kept deliberately.
# Asserted at their existing values purely as a tripwire: if a rebase changes
# any of these, merge_config prints a "redefined" warning and you look.
CONFIG_SCHED_WALT=m
CONFIG_QCOM_MEMLAT=m
CONFIG_CPU_IDLE_GOV_QCOM_LPM=m
CONFIG_DEVFREQ_GOV_QCOM_ADRENO_TZ=m
CONFIG_DEVFREQ_GOV_QCOM_GPUBW_MON=m
CONFIG_QTI_THERMAL_LIMITS_DCVS=m
CONFIG_QTI_CPUFREQ_CDEV=m
CONFIG_QTI_DEVFREQ_CDEV=m

# [have] EAS. Unchanged — schedutil + uclamp + energy model is already right.
CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_DEFAULT_GOV_SCHEDUTIL=y
CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK=y
CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK_GROUP=y
CONFIG_ENERGY_MODEL=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [have] BBR is ALREADY the default in arter97's config — an earlier revision
# of this fragment listed it as an addition, which was wrong. Cubic stays
# compiled in so congestion control remains switchable at runtime.
CONFIG_TCP_CONG_BBR=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_BBR=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_TCP_CONG="bbr"
CONFIG_TCP_CONG_CUBIC=y
CONFIG_NET_SCH_FQ_CODEL=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Codegen — LTO is compiler-gated, verify it survived on the real toolchain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# arter97 uses ThinLTO. The LTO choice is gated on compiler capability checks,
# so it silently degrades to CONFIG_LTO_NONE=y when expanded with a toolchain
# that fails them — the base defconfig alone does this too, so a bare
# `olddefconfig` on the wrong clang is NOT evidence of a fragment problem.
# Do not "fix" it by forcing LTO_CLANG_FULL; that fights the choice block.
# After configuring on the build VM (clang 22), confirm:
#     grep -E '^CONFIG_LTO' .config     # expect LTO_CLANG_THIN=y, not LTO_NONE
CONFIG_LTO_CLANG=y
CONFIG_LTO_CLANG_THIN=y
CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_PERFORMANCE=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mitigations — keep ON
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verified across 17 kernels: nobody disables these, including arter97, and no
# anykernel.sh or boot cmdline sets mitigations=off. The "custom kernels turn
# mitigations off for speed" belief is simply false here.
CONFIG_CPU_MITIGATIONS=y
CONFIG_ARM64_ERRATUM_3194386=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [opt] Hardening that arter97 deliberately REMOVES for speed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is his actual performance strategy, and it is the real answer to "what
# did he change" — not mitigations, not O3. His config has:
#     # CONFIG_CFI_CLANG is not set
#     # CONFIG_SHADOW_CALL_STACK is not set
#     # CONFIG_HARDENED_USERCOPY is not set
#     # CONFIG_KASAN is not set
#     CONFIG_INIT_STACK_NONE=y
#
# Re-enabling costs performance — that is the trade he made on purpose. Two
# of the four below survive a merge as-is; the other two DO NOT and are left
# commented with their real dependency, because silently-dropped hardening is
# worse than no hardening.
CONFIG_SHADOW_CALL_STACK=y
CONFIG_HARDENED_USERCOPY=y
CONFIG_INIT_STACK_ALL_ZERO=y

# CFI_CLANG depends on LTO_CLANG && KALLSYMS, so it disappears wherever LTO
# degraded to LTO_NONE. Enable it only after confirming on the build VM that
# LTO_CLANG_THIN actually survived — otherwise it drops without a word:
# CONFIG_CFI_CLANG=y

# KASAN_HW_TAGS depends on CONFIG_KASAN=y, which arter97 disables — asking for
# HW_TAGS alone is a no-op. Enable the parent too if you want MTE:
# CONFIG_KASAN=y
# CONFIG_KASAN_HW_TAGS=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT changed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HZ stays 250 (arter97, stock, and 16 of 17 surveyed kernels). Zixine alone
# uses 300 with no published benchmark. Change it with a measurement, not a
# hunch.
CONFIG_HZ_250=y
CONFIG_HZ=250
